What is Firestore CRUD Operations?

CRUD stands for:

C → Create
R → Read
U → Update
D → Delete

In Firestore, CRUD operations are used to store and manage data in Firebase.

Example: Cure Connect

Suppose a user books an appointment.

Firestore Collection
appointments
Document
{
  "doctorName": "Dr. John",
  "patientName": "Fouzan",
  "date": "2026-06-21"
}
1. Create Data

Used to save data into Firestore.

FirebaseFirestore.instance
    .collection('users')
    .add({
  'name': 'Fouzan',
  'email': 'fouzan@gmail.com',
});
Interview Answer

Create operation is used to add new documents to a Firestore collection.

2. Read Data

Used to fetch data from Firestore.

var snapshot = await FirebaseFirestore.instance
    .collection('users')
    .get();

for (var doc in snapshot.docs) {
  print(doc['name']);
}
Interview Answer

Read operation is used to retrieve documents from Firestore collections.

3. Update Data

Used to modify existing data.

FirebaseFirestore.instance
    .collection('users')
    .doc(userId)
    .update({
  'name': 'Fouzan P'
});
Interview Answer

Update operation is used to modify existing document fields in Firestore.

4. Delete Data

Used to remove data.

FirebaseFirestore.instance
    .collection('users')
    .doc(userId)
    .delete();
Interview Answer

Delete operation removes a document from a Firestore collection.


In Flutter, both ListView and ListView.builder display lists, but they work differently.

ListView

Creates all items at once.

ListView(
  children: [
    Text("Item 1"),
    Text("Item 2"),
    Text("Item 3"),
  ],
)

✅ Good for:

Small lists
Fixed number of widgets

❌ Problem:

If you have 1000 items, Flutter creates all 1000 widgets immediately.
More memory usage.
Lower performance.

ListView.builder
Creates items only when needed (lazy loading).

ListView.builder(
  itemCount: 1000,
  itemBuilder: (context, index) {
    return ListTile(
      title: Text("Item $index"),
    );
  },
)

✅ Good for:

Large lists
API data
Chat screens
Product lists
Dynamic content

✅ Better performance:

Creates only visible items on the screen.
Saves memory.
Interview Answer

ListView builds all children at once, making it suitable for small static lists.

ListView.builder builds items lazily as they become visible, making it more memory-efficient and suitable for large or dynamic lists.